{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "165",
    "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man with a history of coronary artery disease status post–bypass surgery and emphysema presented with chest pain, dyspnea, cough, night sweats, and a 20-pound unintentional weight loss. Physical examination was unremarkable. Laboratory studies, including a complete blood count and comprehensive metabolic profile, were within normal limits. A noncontrast chest CT revealed a retrocardiac necrotic mediastinal mass measuring 1.2 × 8.6 × 10.7 cm, causing mass effect on the esophagus, left atrium, and pulmonary veins. Transbronchial needle aspiration of a right hilar mass showed benign bronchial cells. EUS-guided FNA of an oval, hypoechoic posterior mediastinal lesion (68 × 70 mm) obtained a core biopsy that demonstrated a spindle cell neoplasm with large nuclei and prominent nucleoli. Immunohistochemical staining was positive for CD117, DOG1, Cam5.2, and AE1/AE3. Somatic genetic analysis of cKIT and PDGFRA revealed no mutations, and germline testing was positive for NF1, suggesting wild-type GIST. Upper endoscopy and colonoscopy identified no other lesions. PET imaging showed FDG uptake in multiple pulmonary nodules, hilar lymph nodes, and a pleural-based lesion on the seventh rib. The patient was started on imatinib.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Leiomyosarcoma\nB. GIST was considered the primary diagnosis because the biopsy “stained positive for CD117, DOG1…\nC. LMS and other smooth muscle tumors were initially thought unlikely because “Other spindle cell tumors of the GI tract…\nD. The diagnosis was revised to high\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
